1
00:00:01,203 --> 00:00:04,133
(We Get Along Pretty Well)

2
00:00:04,333 --> 00:00:06,203
- It's good to see you. - Good to see you.

3
00:00:06,673 --> 00:00:08,342
Gosh, Sung Hwan...

4
00:00:08,343 --> 00:00:11,273
is clearly flaunting to have been to Kazakhstan.

5
00:00:11,513 --> 00:00:13,412
- What kind of style is this? - It's pretty.

6
00:00:13,413 --> 00:00:14,442
- It looks good, right? - Yes.

7
00:00:14,443 --> 00:00:17,553
My shoes and hat are Kazakh traditional.

8
00:00:18,053 --> 00:00:20,483
- Gosh, but... Hold on. - Yes?

9
00:00:21,223 --> 00:00:22,392
A lot of fur falls out from this.

10
00:00:22,393 --> 00:00:24,073
From all over... This is driving me crazy.

11
00:00:24,123 --> 00:00:26,523
It's going into my mouth too. My goodness.

12
00:00:26,723 --> 00:00:28,462
(Taking a glance)

13
00:00:28,463 --> 00:00:29,732
Wait. Please don't come close.

14
00:00:29,733 --> 00:00:31,533
- It'll stick on me. - Oh, you're right.

15
00:00:31,703 --> 00:00:33,333
(Wiping it clean)

16
00:00:33,863 --> 00:00:35,503
- I lint-rolled my coat. - Right, this...

17
00:00:36,633 --> 00:00:38,273
Hey, you're quite cold.

18
00:00:38,343 --> 00:00:39,642
(I'm joking.)

19
00:00:39,643 --> 00:00:43,173
By the way, I heard amazing news from Kazakhstan.

20
00:00:43,713 --> 00:00:45,113
Gosh, our President Jun...

21
00:00:45,513 --> 00:00:47,342
won the Grand Award at KBS!

22
00:00:47,343 --> 00:00:48,782
- Thank you. - Congratulations.

23
00:00:48,783 --> 00:00:50,452
("Jun Hyun Moo's Moving Grand Award Win")

24
00:00:50,453 --> 00:00:51,982
Congratulations.

25
00:00:51,983 --> 00:00:54,423
- We have another good news. - What is it?

26
00:00:54,623 --> 00:00:56,123
Brother, do a high-five with me.

27
00:00:56,593 --> 00:00:58,233
- My Pepper Brother. - My Pepper Brother.

28
00:00:58,293 --> 00:00:59,963
Our war isn't over.

29
00:01:00,793 --> 00:01:01,932
- What? - I mean...

30
00:01:01,933 --> 00:01:04,009
- Oh, the Best Couple Award? - The Best Couple Award.

31
00:01:04,033 --> 00:01:06,163
You were nominated for the Best Couple Award.

32
00:01:06,333 --> 00:01:08,332
Thankfully, us, Pepper Brothers...

33
00:01:08,333 --> 00:01:10,502
were nominated for the Best Couple Award.

34
00:01:10,503 --> 00:01:12,502
You can vote until midnight today,

35
00:01:12,503 --> 00:01:13,772
- so please vote for us, - Pepper Brothers.

36
00:01:13,773 --> 00:01:15,212
Pepper Brothers.

37
00:01:15,213 --> 00:01:16,342
- You look good together. - Please vote for us.

38
00:01:16,343 --> 00:01:17,873
(Smiling)

39
00:01:18,713 --> 00:01:21,213
(Our Sweet Little Family)

40
00:01:23,013 --> 00:01:25,023
The weather isn't nice.

41
00:01:27,183 --> 00:01:28,452
(Kian84 came to the airport while showing off his Japanese skills.)

42
00:01:28,453 --> 00:01:29,893
You're studying hard.

43
00:01:31,353 --> 00:01:33,663
So, it's not very good.

44
00:01:34,863 --> 00:01:37,363
(Trudging while commenting on the cloudy day)

45
00:01:38,403 --> 00:01:39,533
Where are you going?

46
00:01:40,303 --> 00:01:42,603
I went there to pick someone up.

47
00:01:46,073 --> 00:01:48,573
(Looking around)

48
00:01:51,073 --> 00:01:52,183
Mom!

49
00:01:52,843 --> 00:01:54,743
- Mom! - Oh, it's your mom.

50
00:01:55,183 --> 00:01:56,913
- Oh, she came from Jeju. - Yes.

51
00:01:58,283 --> 00:01:59,352
You must be hungry.

52
00:01:59,353 --> 00:02:02,423
(Whenever Kian84 visits Jeju Island, )

53
00:02:03,493 --> 00:02:05,663
(she prepares a feast for him as he's working hard.)

54
00:02:05,863 --> 00:02:06,922
Goodness.

55
00:02:06,923 --> 00:02:08,992
(She always cares for her son sincerely.)

56
00:02:08,993 --> 00:02:10,762
- Mother... - Be careful.

57
00:02:10,763 --> 00:02:12,302
(A sweet mom who cheers Kian84 on right by his side)

58
00:02:12,303 --> 00:02:13,803
- Bye, Mom. - I'm leaving. Bye.

59
00:02:14,263 --> 00:02:16,133
It's Madam Yoon Kyung Mi's...

60
00:02:16,333 --> 00:02:17,432
sixty...

61
00:02:17,433 --> 00:02:18,872
(Thinking)

62
00:02:18,873 --> 00:02:21,002
It's her 65th birthday,

63
00:02:21,003 --> 00:02:23,413
so I went to pick her up.

64
00:02:24,313 --> 00:02:25,313
Mom!

65
00:02:25,313 --> 00:02:26,313
(Mom, come over to this side.)

66
00:02:26,314 --> 00:02:27,913
- It's still red light. - Right.

67
00:02:29,713 --> 00:02:32,722
(Staring into the distance after awkwardly calling her)

68
00:02:32,723 --> 00:02:34,383
Oh, you haven't seen your mom yet?

69
00:02:34,893 --> 00:02:36,493
It was awkward to keep looking at her...

70
00:02:36,623 --> 00:02:38,462
- after saying hi. - Oh, is that so?

71
00:02:38,463 --> 00:02:39,592
After I went, "Mom..."

72
00:02:39,593 --> 00:02:42,092
It's hard for only sons to stare intently at their moms.

73
00:02:42,093 --> 00:02:43,692
- Is that so? - "Hi." And we'd look elsewhere.

74
00:02:43,693 --> 00:02:45,632
Who does that? Why do you keep...

75
00:02:45,633 --> 00:02:47,402
When you see your mom across the street,

76
00:02:47,403 --> 00:02:49,302
do you keep smiling at her while looking?

77
00:02:49,303 --> 00:02:51,032
- Yes. When you see... - I don't do that, but...

78
00:02:51,033 --> 00:02:52,972
your mom and dad, don't you go like this?

79
00:02:52,973 --> 00:02:54,772
- "Mom!" - That's why you need a daughter.

80
00:02:54,773 --> 00:02:56,042
(This is why you need a daughter like Joy in this world.)

81
00:02:56,043 --> 00:02:57,413
Sons can't do that.

82
00:02:58,073 --> 00:03:00,433
(The only son only looks at the ground instead of his mom.)

83
00:03:01,613 --> 00:03:03,183
(Rushing)

84
00:03:03,513 --> 00:03:04,993
- Gosh, it's so heavy. - What's that?

85
00:03:05,283 --> 00:03:06,752
- Goodness. - What did you bring?

86
00:03:06,753 --> 00:03:07,883
Your face...

87
00:03:08,793 --> 00:03:11,493
- What? - You look so worn out.

88
00:03:12,463 --> 00:03:14,322
- What? - You don't look so good.

89
00:03:14,323 --> 00:03:16,533
(You don't look so good.)

90
00:03:16,933 --> 00:03:18,533
What does that mean?

91
00:03:18,863 --> 00:03:20,679
- It's my first time hearing it. - "It's bad."

92
00:03:20,703 --> 00:03:21,772
It's a Gyeongsang Province dialect.

93
00:03:21,773 --> 00:03:23,072
- It's from Gyeongsang Province? - It is.

94
00:03:23,073 --> 00:03:24,773
It means it's not so good.

95
00:03:25,503 --> 00:03:27,272
- I just learned it. - "It's bad?"

96
00:03:27,273 --> 00:03:28,672
Then what about when she told you that?

97
00:03:28,673 --> 00:03:30,273
(What about when his mom told him that?)

98
00:03:31,213 --> 00:03:32,783
- He didn't listen. - Right.

99
00:03:33,013 --> 00:03:34,483
I don't listen to her much.

100
00:03:36,013 --> 00:03:38,322
When our moms talk,

101
00:03:38,323 --> 00:03:39,823
we'd get the gist of it...

102
00:03:40,083 --> 00:03:42,092
and don't ask about the unimportant stuff.

103
00:03:42,093 --> 00:03:43,493
Because the conversation gets long.

104
00:03:44,623 --> 00:03:46,183
- Why did you pack so much? - I mean...

105
00:03:46,293 --> 00:03:48,963
I brought green onion kimchi. I made some kimchi.

106
00:03:49,693 --> 00:03:51,663
I have lots of kimchi at home.

107
00:03:52,103 --> 00:03:54,562
There's something on your face.

108
00:03:54,563 --> 00:03:56,003
Here?

109
00:03:56,273 --> 00:03:57,502
You do things like that too?

110
00:03:57,503 --> 00:03:58,843
I do that.

111
00:03:59,973 --> 00:04:01,942
(Staring)

112
00:04:01,943 --> 00:04:04,772
You look so rough.

113
00:04:04,773 --> 00:04:06,643
- What? - You didn't wash your face?

114
00:04:07,083 --> 00:04:08,812
- I did. - You washed your face?

115
00:04:08,813 --> 00:04:10,482
- She knows him well. - I did in the morning.

116
00:04:10,483 --> 00:04:11,483
Okay.

117
00:04:11,683 --> 00:04:13,853
(Running away to the taxi that arrived just in time)

118
00:04:15,323 --> 00:04:16,653
Should I put this in there too?

119
00:04:17,823 --> 00:04:19,522
- It should be placed upright. - Why?

120
00:04:19,523 --> 00:04:21,043
- It should be placed upright. - This?

121
00:04:21,093 --> 00:04:22,332
This doesn't have to be upright.

122
00:04:22,333 --> 00:04:24,173
- But this one should be. - I'll get in first.

123
00:04:25,603 --> 00:04:27,663
(I'll get in first.)

124
00:04:28,003 --> 00:04:29,072
- Goodness. - Hello.

125
00:04:29,073 --> 00:04:31,533
(He sets off with his mom in a taxi.)

126
00:04:32,173 --> 00:04:34,103
You look so rough.

127
00:04:34,203 --> 00:04:35,513
- What? - Your face...

128
00:04:36,513 --> 00:04:37,542
(Her son's skin keeps drawing her attention even in the taxi.)

129
00:04:37,543 --> 00:04:38,782
- How many times has it been? - Your face...

130
00:04:38,783 --> 00:04:39,843
It's all right.

131
00:04:39,943 --> 00:04:41,112
You should apply some cream.

132
00:04:41,113 --> 00:04:42,712
It's rough because I'm old.

133
00:04:42,713 --> 00:04:45,553
- No, it's not. Your face... - It's okay.

134
00:04:45,653 --> 00:04:48,953
It's so funny because it hits close to home.

135
00:04:49,423 --> 00:04:51,052
(Don't eat flour-based food.)

136
00:04:51,053 --> 00:04:52,092
(Be careful.)

137
00:04:52,093 --> 00:04:53,493
(Diabetes is a chronic disease.)

138
00:04:53,893 --> 00:04:55,932
You should take care of your skin too.

139
00:04:55,933 --> 00:04:56,992
- Well, I... - You're still single.

140
00:04:56,993 --> 00:04:58,092
You shouldn't look like that.

141
00:04:58,093 --> 00:04:59,332
I've seen a dermatologist a few times,

142
00:04:59,333 --> 00:05:00,562
but it doesn't change much.

143
00:05:00,563 --> 00:05:01,703
But still...

144
00:05:01,933 --> 00:05:05,142
Your skin ages in no time.

145
00:05:05,143 --> 00:05:06,703
You're a lot older now.

146
00:05:07,303 --> 00:05:08,713
That wasn't a rap, was it?

147
00:05:09,073 --> 00:05:10,313
That wasn't a rap, was it?

148
00:05:11,413 --> 00:05:12,613
I thought she was a rapper.

149
00:05:13,083 --> 00:05:14,343
Please drop a beat.

150
00:05:15,013 --> 00:05:16,682
You can't just go once.

151
00:05:16,683 --> 00:05:18,923
You need to see one continuously.

152
00:05:19,423 --> 00:05:20,722
A single man should have...

153
00:05:20,723 --> 00:05:22,452
- She's starting. - shiny skin...

154
00:05:22,453 --> 00:05:23,852
to meet ladies.

155
00:05:23,853 --> 00:05:25,763
- She passed. - She'd get a pass for that.

156
00:05:26,093 --> 00:05:27,692
The producers would get intimidated instead.

157
00:05:27,693 --> 00:05:29,163
(Because she's so talented)

158
00:05:29,463 --> 00:05:32,233
You should apply some cream. Other people...

159
00:05:32,363 --> 00:05:34,262
have shiny skin.

160
00:05:34,263 --> 00:05:35,272
What?

161
00:05:35,273 --> 00:05:38,502
- You need to put on some cream. - Okay.

162
00:05:38,503 --> 00:05:40,183
(A nag-like rap filled with motherly love)

163
00:05:40,243 --> 00:05:43,513
Normally, when moms keep nagging like that,

164
00:05:43,673 --> 00:05:45,212
children would go, "Stop!"

165
00:05:45,213 --> 00:05:46,642
- I do too. - But you don't.

166
00:05:46,643 --> 00:05:49,552
- I think of it as her folk song. - Is that so?

167
00:05:49,553 --> 00:05:51,083
- It's a folk song? - That's right.

168
00:05:51,823 --> 00:05:53,382
When we were young,

169
00:05:53,383 --> 00:05:56,052
we talked back to everything like it's "100 Minute Debate."

170
00:05:56,053 --> 00:05:57,092
It's pointless.

171
00:05:57,093 --> 00:05:59,022
Moms must say whatever they want.

172
00:05:59,023 --> 00:06:00,823
- You can't stop them. - You can't.

173
00:06:00,923 --> 00:06:03,493
- If you refute, she refutes too. - Yes, it keeps going.

174
00:06:03,863 --> 00:06:05,432
"Okay." That's the best answer.

175
00:06:05,433 --> 00:06:06,633
(Quick acceptance is the way.)

176
00:06:07,503 --> 00:06:09,202
- You didn't have breakfast at all? - No.

177
00:06:09,203 --> 00:06:13,173
I felt rushed in the morning.

178
00:06:13,773 --> 00:06:15,813
I cried a bit while looking at Candy's photos.

179
00:06:15,973 --> 00:06:17,012
The dog.

180
00:06:17,013 --> 00:06:20,813
"Candy, when I bring your younger siblings,"

181
00:06:20,983 --> 00:06:23,413
"treat them well so they wouldn't hurt."

182
00:06:23,653 --> 00:06:26,753
I kept praying to Candy like that.

183
00:06:27,423 --> 00:06:28,493
Well...

184
00:06:30,653 --> 00:06:32,763
The dog we had, Candy...

185
00:06:33,263 --> 00:06:34,393
Has it been 13 years?

186
00:06:34,723 --> 00:06:37,833
We adopted the dog when I was 30.

187
00:06:38,393 --> 00:06:39,533
That dog...

188
00:06:40,733 --> 00:06:42,803
went over the Rainbow Bridge.

189
00:06:43,973 --> 00:06:47,202
(Candy welcomed him when he visited his mom's house in Jeju.)

190
00:06:47,203 --> 00:06:48,813
- Candy. - You brat.

191
00:06:50,013 --> 00:06:52,782
(It acted like it's savage, )

192
00:06:52,783 --> 00:06:53,843
It was savage.

193
00:06:54,083 --> 00:06:57,383
(but Candy was always by its owner's side.)

194
00:06:58,853 --> 00:07:00,922
(Our youngest who was with us for 13 years)

195
00:07:00,923 --> 00:07:02,192
The dog you mentioned...

196
00:07:02,193 --> 00:07:03,422
- in your acceptance speech? - That's right.

197
00:07:03,423 --> 00:07:05,143
(Our youngest who was with us for 13 years)

198
00:07:05,423 --> 00:07:06,992
(He was worried about Candy during his acceptance speech...)

199
00:07:06,993 --> 00:07:08,593
(on "2022 MBC Entertainment Awards.")

200
00:07:09,393 --> 00:07:11,933
(The dog is quite old now.)

201
00:07:14,103 --> 00:07:18,173
It died peacefully in my mom's arms.

202
00:07:18,403 --> 00:07:21,843
I mentioned it during my acceptance speech,

203
00:07:22,173 --> 00:07:24,372
but some people said it was a little overboard...

204
00:07:24,373 --> 00:07:26,612
to mention it during an acceptance speech.

205
00:07:26,613 --> 00:07:28,082
- No way. It was your family. - To me,

206
00:07:28,083 --> 00:07:29,552
- it was like my family. - Of course.

207
00:07:29,553 --> 00:07:31,013
To Mom, it must've meant much more.

208
00:07:31,683 --> 00:07:34,023
With Candy around, she'd talk to it,

209
00:07:34,123 --> 00:07:36,923
feed it, go for a walk, and everything.

210
00:07:37,053 --> 00:07:40,423
She had a daily routine with it, so it was nice.

211
00:07:40,693 --> 00:07:42,163
But with Candy gone,

212
00:07:42,533 --> 00:07:43,892
she suffered from...

213
00:07:43,893 --> 00:07:45,302
- pet loss syndrome. - It's not easy.

214
00:07:45,303 --> 00:07:48,633
From what I saw, she seemed really down.

215
00:07:49,333 --> 00:07:51,973
I can really relate as a fellow dog owner.

216
00:07:52,273 --> 00:07:54,202
It terrifies me to even think about it.

217
00:07:54,203 --> 00:07:55,472
- Imagining her dying like Candy. - Right.

218
00:07:55,473 --> 00:07:57,612
- Kkotbun is already ten, so... - I'm worried about you too.

219
00:07:57,613 --> 00:07:58,712
- Me too. - Right.

220
00:07:58,713 --> 00:08:00,883
I can't see such sad videos...

221
00:08:01,013 --> 00:08:02,883
because it already scares me.

222
00:08:04,253 --> 00:08:06,382
My dog, Toto, also went over the Rainbow Bridge.

223
00:08:06,383 --> 00:08:07,553
- I know. - Right.

224
00:08:07,853 --> 00:08:09,293
I suffered a lot too.

225
00:08:09,523 --> 00:08:11,192
- You too? - It was really hard.

226
00:08:11,193 --> 00:08:12,763
It suddenly pops into my mind.

227
00:08:12,923 --> 00:08:14,692
- That's right. - I go about my life,

228
00:08:14,693 --> 00:08:16,333
but I'd suddenly think of it and tear up.

229
00:08:16,463 --> 00:08:18,863
When my dog, Pookie, went over the Rainbow Bridge,

230
00:08:19,403 --> 00:08:21,703
- it was really hard. - It's really hard.

231
00:08:22,773 --> 00:08:26,203
After 4 to 5 months after Candy died,

232
00:08:26,573 --> 00:08:28,843
she came to her senses.

233
00:08:29,943 --> 00:08:31,672
She said it'd be better to have dogs,

234
00:08:31,673 --> 00:08:35,113
so today, I went with her...

235
00:08:35,813 --> 00:08:39,883
to see if we could adopt stray dogs.

236
00:08:40,683 --> 00:08:42,893
Adopting dogs... But when I...

237
00:08:43,093 --> 00:08:45,374
told her to adopt one quickly if it was too hard for her,

238
00:08:45,423 --> 00:08:48,292
she said she felt bad for Candy.

239
00:08:48,293 --> 00:08:49,613
My mom said the exact same thing.

240
00:08:49,793 --> 00:08:51,762
She said it felt like she was replacing it with another dog...

241
00:08:51,763 --> 00:08:53,102
like it's an object.

242
00:08:53,103 --> 00:08:55,533
- That's right. - So she wasn't going to adopt one,

243
00:08:56,703 --> 00:08:58,743
but she lives alone on Jeju Island.

244
00:08:58,943 --> 00:09:00,442
When she wakes up, Candy isn't there,

245
00:09:00,443 --> 00:09:02,242
so it's hard for her every morning.

246
00:09:02,243 --> 00:09:03,243
That's right.

247
00:09:03,743 --> 00:09:05,642
She feels lonely too. She has to eat alone.

248
00:09:05,643 --> 00:09:06,989
- So... - She's reminded of it more.

249
00:09:07,013 --> 00:09:09,452
We decided that it'd be better if she had a dog.

250
00:09:09,453 --> 00:09:11,783
That's why we went to adopt a dog.

251
00:09:12,653 --> 00:09:15,423
Candy was so adorable.

252
00:09:16,153 --> 00:09:17,823
Any dog adopted by you would be lucky.

253
00:09:18,053 --> 00:09:21,363
I treat dogs well. I treat them like a precious treasure.

254
00:09:21,693 --> 00:09:23,663
I hope it comes to me like fate.

255
00:09:23,993 --> 00:09:26,533
- I should raise it well. - Right.

256
00:09:26,663 --> 00:09:27,963
She got a bit emotional.

257
00:09:31,103 --> 00:09:32,543
What's with the weather?

258
00:09:32,873 --> 00:09:34,402
It's cloudy today.

259
00:09:34,403 --> 00:09:36,143
It didn't say it'd rain.

260
00:09:36,913 --> 00:09:38,613
So do you eat carbs these days?

261
00:09:39,083 --> 00:09:40,543
I eat whatever I want.

262
00:09:41,013 --> 00:09:43,583
- Because you work out so much. - Work out?

263
00:09:44,453 --> 00:09:46,252
(Staring outside the window for no reason)

264
00:09:46,253 --> 00:09:47,973
There must be something outside the window.

265
00:09:48,183 --> 00:09:50,293
My prayer has changed these days.

266
00:09:50,423 --> 00:09:53,193
- "Please let my son marry well." - These days, guys my age...

267
00:09:53,863 --> 00:09:55,833
Guys in their 40s work out hard.

268
00:09:56,033 --> 00:09:57,713
People don't like it if we don't work out.

269
00:09:57,963 --> 00:10:00,563
When I meet my friends these days, they would...

270
00:10:00,633 --> 00:10:01,962
- It's "Show Me the Money." - talk about their sons-in-law,

271
00:10:01,963 --> 00:10:03,572
- Here it is. Here's the beat. - their daughters-in-law...

272
00:10:03,573 --> 00:10:05,472
That's the only thing they talk about. Their grandchildren...

273
00:10:05,473 --> 00:10:08,573
They're all younger than you.

274
00:10:08,713 --> 00:10:10,343
They're in their mid-30s.

275
00:10:11,073 --> 00:10:12,612
They got married early.

276
00:10:12,613 --> 00:10:13,983
They all have children.

277
00:10:14,083 --> 00:10:15,382
Why would you talk about sons-in-law...

278
00:10:15,383 --> 00:10:16,943
when we were talking about working out?

279
00:10:17,813 --> 00:10:19,323
So when I...

280
00:10:20,223 --> 00:10:21,823
talk with others,

281
00:10:22,093 --> 00:10:26,093
I'd wonder, "Will my son even get married?"

282
00:10:26,223 --> 00:10:27,823
I will. Don't worry.

283
00:10:28,623 --> 00:10:30,432
- You should get married. - I should.

284
00:10:30,433 --> 00:10:32,332
It's the exact same repertoire as my family's.

285
00:10:32,333 --> 00:10:34,502
You gave a satisfying answer for your mom.

286
00:10:34,503 --> 00:10:36,579
- Right. It's hard to say that too. - He does everything.

287
00:10:36,603 --> 00:10:37,733
- Yes. - Is it there?

288
00:10:38,103 --> 00:10:39,142
Please stop here, sir.

289
00:10:39,143 --> 00:10:40,342
(They arrived somewhere before Mommynem starts again.)

290
00:10:40,343 --> 00:10:41,603
It's here, Mom.

291
00:10:42,373 --> 00:10:44,243
(I'll carry the bags.)

292
00:10:45,613 --> 00:10:48,083
Gosh, you're carrying all the bags.

293
00:10:48,583 --> 00:10:49,612
- It's heavy, right? - That's the least I could do.

294
00:10:49,613 --> 00:10:50,682
- This one's heavy, right? - It's all right.

295
00:10:50,683 --> 00:10:52,483
- We're good at carrying stuff. - Yes.

296
00:10:53,323 --> 00:10:55,523
You'd be a jerk if you let your mom carry that.

297
00:10:57,623 --> 00:10:59,622
It must be here. Why is it so well-managed?

298
00:10:59,623 --> 00:11:00,663
(Impressed)

299
00:11:01,063 --> 00:11:02,193
Hello.

300
00:11:02,733 --> 00:11:04,792
- Hello. - Where is that?

301
00:11:04,793 --> 00:11:06,502
- Hello. - Hello.

302
00:11:06,503 --> 00:11:08,503
- Goodness, baby. - Do we have to put on slippers?

303
00:11:08,603 --> 00:11:11,373
Goodness, baby. My gosh.

304
00:11:11,673 --> 00:11:14,603
My goodness. There are pretty babies here.

305
00:11:14,873 --> 00:11:15,942
Baby.

306
00:11:15,943 --> 00:11:18,513
- Oh, gosh. - Baby.

307
00:11:18,613 --> 00:11:19,742
Look at this guy.

308
00:11:19,743 --> 00:11:21,213
- Goodness. - Gosh.

309
00:11:21,943 --> 00:11:24,053
- Goodness. - How pretty.

310
00:11:24,413 --> 00:11:27,382
A friend of mine from a community service...

311
00:11:27,383 --> 00:11:28,723
introduced me to this place.

312
00:11:29,223 --> 00:11:31,793
This is a pet grooming school.

313
00:11:32,053 --> 00:11:33,863
The director of the place...

314
00:11:34,223 --> 00:11:37,433
thought stray dogs needed to be groomed,

315
00:11:37,593 --> 00:11:39,102
- Right. To be chosen. - so they can...

316
00:11:39,103 --> 00:11:42,132
be adopted to a good place.

317
00:11:42,133 --> 00:11:43,432
So she does that.

318
00:11:43,433 --> 00:11:44,702
That's really important, though.

319
00:11:44,703 --> 00:11:45,742
They need to be well-groomed...

320
00:11:45,743 --> 00:11:46,802
- to be chosen. - That's right.

321
00:11:46,803 --> 00:11:47,972
- That's right. - You said they needed...

322
00:11:47,973 --> 00:11:49,212
pretty photos to be adopted well.

323
00:11:49,213 --> 00:11:50,313
- That's right. - Right.

324
00:11:50,813 --> 00:11:54,343
(A neat appearance is important for stray dog adoption.)

325
00:11:54,943 --> 00:11:56,112
At the center where I do community service,

326
00:11:56,113 --> 00:11:57,282
there are scruffy dogs.

327
00:11:57,283 --> 00:11:58,952
After washing and grooming them,

328
00:11:58,953 --> 00:12:00,423
- we'd take nice photos. - Right.

329
00:12:00,553 --> 00:12:01,682
We posted the photos, and they got adopted abroad...

330
00:12:01,683 --> 00:12:02,853
to the US, Germany and such.

331
00:12:02,993 --> 00:12:04,422
- It can't be helped. - That's right.

332
00:12:04,423 --> 00:12:05,522
- Of course. - Yes.

333
00:12:05,523 --> 00:12:07,562
I was honestly surprised...

334
00:12:07,563 --> 00:12:09,593
because the dogs there were well-groomed.

335
00:12:09,733 --> 00:12:12,902
There are many adoption centers where dogs aren't well-groomed...

336
00:12:12,903 --> 00:12:14,603
because there are just so many.

337
00:12:15,063 --> 00:12:18,533
With a hope to find good owners,

338
00:12:18,673 --> 00:12:20,843
they must've groomed them well.

339
00:12:21,273 --> 00:12:23,242
- Gosh, that punk. - They'd groom and keep them neat.

340
00:12:23,243 --> 00:12:25,743
My mom used to...

341
00:12:26,043 --> 00:12:27,512
have two dogs.

342
00:12:27,513 --> 00:12:29,713
One was Gongju,

343
00:12:29,853 --> 00:12:32,183
and the other one was Candy. Those were the two.

344
00:12:32,383 --> 00:12:35,122
Since then, she has volunteered...

345
00:12:35,123 --> 00:12:37,623
at dog rescue centers from time to time.

346
00:12:37,793 --> 00:12:39,923
Since my mom knows how rescue centers run,

347
00:12:40,263 --> 00:12:42,493
we went to adopt dogs there.

348
00:12:42,763 --> 00:12:44,933
Goodness. It likes people.

349
00:12:45,163 --> 00:12:46,662
Gosh, baby.

350
00:12:46,663 --> 00:12:48,732
- That one looks like Candy. - It looks exactly like Candy.

351
00:12:48,733 --> 00:12:50,903
There, there.

352
00:12:51,273 --> 00:12:52,902
- Hello. - See? Your mom likes it.

353
00:12:52,903 --> 00:12:54,602
- She can't help but be drawn to it. - Come here.

354
00:12:54,603 --> 00:12:56,513
- How cute. - There, there. Yes.

355
00:12:56,743 --> 00:12:58,443
Come here.

356
00:12:58,913 --> 00:13:01,242
- Come here. Baby. - It looks like Candy.

357
00:13:01,243 --> 00:13:02,852
The one that looks exactly like Candy went to my mom.

358
00:13:02,853 --> 00:13:05,083
How cute. Goodness.

359
00:13:05,623 --> 00:13:06,722
There, there.

360
00:13:06,723 --> 00:13:07,753
How sweet.

361
00:13:07,853 --> 00:13:09,992
- It's like a baby. - Sweet baby.

362
00:13:09,993 --> 00:13:11,022
These days,

363
00:13:11,023 --> 00:13:12,392
- You're so sweet. - after COVID-19,

364
00:13:12,393 --> 00:13:15,162
- There, there. - people travel a lot.

365
00:13:15,163 --> 00:13:16,993
Where did that sweet thing come from?

366
00:13:17,563 --> 00:13:20,562
This one was rescued...

367
00:13:20,563 --> 00:13:22,163
after a pet store closed down.

368
00:13:22,333 --> 00:13:24,833
It had a tumor...

369
00:13:25,103 --> 00:13:27,772
in its tongue,

370
00:13:27,773 --> 00:13:29,972
so we had it removed.

371
00:13:29,973 --> 00:13:32,582
It hasn't been long since it had the surgery,

372
00:13:32,583 --> 00:13:34,012
so it couldn't be adopted.

373
00:13:34,013 --> 00:13:36,182
- Now, it's up for adoption. - Every dog has its story.

374
00:13:36,183 --> 00:13:38,052
- There, there. - Its name is Nana.

375
00:13:38,053 --> 00:13:39,882
- It's around nine months. - It's quite young.

376
00:13:39,883 --> 00:13:41,553
- It's very young. - It's still a baby.

377
00:13:41,653 --> 00:13:43,022
Look at the way it's looking at his mom.

378
00:13:43,023 --> 00:13:45,052
- There, there. - Goodness.

379
00:13:45,053 --> 00:13:46,613
Gosh, look at the dogs approaching him.

380
00:13:46,763 --> 00:13:47,823
Mashi.

381
00:13:47,923 --> 00:13:49,483
- How cute. - Are adoptions going well?

382
00:13:49,533 --> 00:13:51,892
- Not really. No. - It's not going well?

383
00:13:51,893 --> 00:13:53,633
Because the economy is bad,

384
00:13:53,933 --> 00:13:56,472
there are way more people giving up their dogs.

385
00:13:56,473 --> 00:13:58,572
There are fewer people willing to raise one.

386
00:13:58,573 --> 00:13:59,602
- Goodness. - Yes.

387
00:13:59,603 --> 00:14:00,942
- It's a difficult problem. - Not many can be adopted.

388
00:14:00,943 --> 00:14:02,183
What do we do with all of them?

389
00:14:02,643 --> 00:14:05,072
If there aren't any inquiries for adoption...

390
00:14:05,073 --> 00:14:07,613
and if they stay here for too long,

391
00:14:07,843 --> 00:14:09,882
I look for adopters abroad.

392
00:14:09,883 --> 00:14:11,383
If the dogs are adopted abroad,

393
00:14:11,713 --> 00:14:13,929
do the adopters pay for the plane tickets and everything?

394
00:14:13,953 --> 00:14:15,593
- We pay for everything. - What?

395
00:14:15,923 --> 00:14:17,993
- Yes. - Who pays for that?

396
00:14:18,753 --> 00:14:20,762
- That's why people donate. - We do.

397
00:14:20,763 --> 00:14:21,792
- I pay for it. - I see.

398
00:14:21,793 --> 00:14:23,513
- That's where it's used. - You pay for it?

399
00:14:23,633 --> 00:14:24,732
You pay for it?

400
00:14:24,733 --> 00:14:26,632
- She does a good deed. - Yes.

401
00:14:26,633 --> 00:14:29,373
Doesn't that mean you only spend money?

402
00:14:29,573 --> 00:14:31,579
- You need to pay the rent too. - That's true, but...

403
00:14:31,603 --> 00:14:34,643
Then you should make donations too.

404
00:14:35,243 --> 00:14:38,013
- My goodness. - But I think...

405
00:14:38,243 --> 00:14:41,382
it's a matter of where I focus on spending my money.

406
00:14:41,383 --> 00:14:44,853
The money is ought to be spent anyway.

407
00:14:45,083 --> 00:14:46,552
If I save money on one thing,

408
00:14:46,553 --> 00:14:47,722
I can save these guys.

409
00:14:47,723 --> 00:14:49,552
- I can save another life. - Exactly.

410
00:14:49,553 --> 00:14:51,552
- So where I spend the money... - You can never do that...

411
00:14:51,553 --> 00:14:52,622
if you don't love dogs.

412
00:14:52,623 --> 00:14:54,062
- How cute. - You can never.

413
00:14:54,063 --> 00:14:55,792
- You can never do it. - It's snuggling into my arms.

414
00:14:55,793 --> 00:14:57,793
(His mom is deeply in love with the lovely dogs.)

415
00:14:58,163 --> 00:14:59,932
- You like to kiss. - That one's cute too.

416
00:14:59,933 --> 00:15:01,773
- So you like to kiss. - That guy is cute too.

417
00:15:02,803 --> 00:15:05,002
(Filled with love)

418
00:15:05,003 --> 00:15:06,503
My goodness.

419
00:15:07,743 --> 00:15:08,943
How adorable.

420
00:15:09,343 --> 00:15:10,843
There, there.

421
00:15:11,913 --> 00:15:13,082
Baby.

422
00:15:13,083 --> 00:15:14,983
(Kian84 is quietly lost in thought.)

423
00:15:16,683 --> 00:15:18,412
Every time I see stray dogs,

424
00:15:18,413 --> 00:15:21,653
I don't feel easy, to be really honest.

425
00:15:21,923 --> 00:15:24,623
They're so pitiful. They can't even speak.

426
00:15:25,123 --> 00:15:26,422
Yet, they like people.

427
00:15:26,423 --> 00:15:28,623
They wag their tails at people because they like us.

428
00:15:28,693 --> 00:15:31,262
- That's what's really sad. - The ones that approach me...

429
00:15:31,263 --> 00:15:33,263
are also pitiful because they're so adorable.

430
00:15:33,903 --> 00:15:37,303
But those that can't approach me are also pitiful.

431
00:15:38,173 --> 00:15:40,743
Maybe they...

432
00:15:41,003 --> 00:15:43,413
don't trust people. Or maybe...

433
00:15:43,673 --> 00:15:45,843
With a timid character like that,

434
00:15:46,113 --> 00:15:48,842
that dog would have a hard time meeting a new owner.

435
00:15:48,843 --> 00:15:50,923
- It's just my thought, but... - I get how you feel.

436
00:15:51,153 --> 00:15:53,053
Of course, I don't feel that good.

437
00:15:54,653 --> 00:15:57,323
You care deeply about such things.

438
00:15:57,393 --> 00:15:59,893
We went to eat together once,

439
00:16:00,063 --> 00:16:01,592
and there was a fish in the fish tank.

440
00:16:01,593 --> 00:16:03,162
He wanted to save the fish,

441
00:16:03,163 --> 00:16:04,283
so he asked how much it was.

442
00:16:04,463 --> 00:16:06,462
He wanted to buy it and release it in the river.

443
00:16:06,463 --> 00:16:08,062
- It'd die right away. - If he releases it,

444
00:16:08,063 --> 00:16:09,673
it get caught again.

445
00:16:10,033 --> 00:16:11,913
I know I went overboard, but this happened too.

446
00:16:11,973 --> 00:16:14,042
I went to have grilled clams on Jebu Island,

447
00:16:14,043 --> 00:16:16,043
and I felt so bad for the clams.

448
00:16:16,473 --> 00:16:18,742
I took them to a mudflat...

449
00:16:18,743 --> 00:16:20,282
and buried them after digging the mud.

450
00:16:20,283 --> 00:16:21,782
There are times when you feel that way.

451
00:16:21,783 --> 00:16:22,813
That's right.

452
00:16:23,883 --> 00:16:26,582
(He just feels bad for the dogs.)

453
00:16:26,583 --> 00:16:28,022
- There, there. - Actually, their teeth...

454
00:16:28,023 --> 00:16:29,452
need to be developed a lot.

455
00:16:29,453 --> 00:16:30,623
(Passing through)

456
00:16:31,093 --> 00:16:32,922
Their teeth fall out because they eat dog food.

457
00:16:32,923 --> 00:16:34,222
This is so nice.

458
00:16:34,223 --> 00:16:37,293
- That's right. - It approached him suddenly.

459
00:16:37,433 --> 00:16:38,692
It came to my side.

460
00:16:38,693 --> 00:16:39,762
(The cotton ball came to Kian84's side.)

461
00:16:39,763 --> 00:16:41,402
It looks like Kkotbun.

462
00:16:41,403 --> 00:16:42,903
(Fluffy like Kkotbun)

463
00:16:43,503 --> 00:16:45,349
- You're so sweet. - It's snuggling into my arms.

464
00:16:45,373 --> 00:16:46,603
(Patting)

465
00:16:47,043 --> 00:16:48,103
Baby.

466
00:16:48,703 --> 00:16:50,142
(Sniffing)

467
00:16:50,143 --> 00:16:52,943
- Well, then... - How cute. That dog is really cute.

468
00:16:53,043 --> 00:16:54,312
That guy is cute.

469
00:16:54,313 --> 00:16:55,833
Is there a dog you're growing fond of?

470
00:16:56,013 --> 00:16:58,452
That dog looks too much like Candy.

471
00:16:58,453 --> 00:16:59,952
Look at that. It's sitting right next to him.

472
00:16:59,953 --> 00:17:01,483
- Actually, I... - Yes?

473
00:17:01,583 --> 00:17:05,423
- I've had two dogs. - Yes.

474
00:17:05,593 --> 00:17:09,132
Thinking back, two dogs played with each other well.

475
00:17:09,133 --> 00:17:11,092
- What? - It's like a baby. It's sleeping.

476
00:17:11,093 --> 00:17:12,632
- What? - It's sleeping.

477
00:17:12,633 --> 00:17:14,353
- It's their first encounter, and... - Yes.

478
00:17:14,433 --> 00:17:15,462
It showed its belly. It's game over.

479
00:17:15,463 --> 00:17:16,502
It has a great personality.

480
00:17:16,503 --> 00:17:20,502
It's not easy for dogs to show their bellies,

481
00:17:20,503 --> 00:17:22,003
unless they feel really comfortable.

482
00:17:22,873 --> 00:17:25,172
I think this one is meant for us.

483
00:17:25,173 --> 00:17:27,083
- It's lying next to me. - The one next to you?

484
00:17:27,383 --> 00:17:29,252
This one is really shy around strangers.

485
00:17:29,253 --> 00:17:30,482
- Really? - Yes.

486
00:17:30,483 --> 00:17:33,423
This one realized it could survive with me.

487
00:17:34,253 --> 00:17:35,792
Is that so?

488
00:17:35,793 --> 00:17:38,423
Look at it. It's sitting right next to me.

489
00:17:38,653 --> 00:17:40,762
- Is that so? - You're right. I was holding it.

490
00:17:40,763 --> 00:17:42,362
It's so pretty.

491
00:17:42,363 --> 00:17:44,392
I think you two have a similar disposition.

492
00:17:44,393 --> 00:17:45,803
An INTP-type.

493
00:17:47,233 --> 00:17:50,503
(When the rescuer was holding it earlier...)

494
00:17:50,903 --> 00:17:52,403
It's only looking at you.

495
00:17:52,503 --> 00:17:54,043
You're right. That punk.

496
00:17:55,273 --> 00:17:57,442
(When it had a chance...)

497
00:17:57,443 --> 00:17:59,612
- Oh, my. Goodness. - Oh, my.

498
00:17:59,613 --> 00:18:01,243
It approached him first.

499
00:18:01,683 --> 00:18:03,353
I guess it likes the smell of your body.

500
00:18:03,953 --> 00:18:05,782
- It's sitting right next to you. - It's right here.

501
00:18:05,783 --> 00:18:07,153
It wants me to take it.

502
00:18:07,183 --> 00:18:08,752
The look in that dog's eyes...

503
00:18:08,753 --> 00:18:11,252
My mom once had a dog...

504
00:18:11,253 --> 00:18:12,862
- that was disowned four times. - Yes.

505
00:18:12,863 --> 00:18:15,062
- Gongju. - It has the same look in the eyes.

506
00:18:15,063 --> 00:18:16,132
- That one. - The other one?

507
00:18:16,133 --> 00:18:17,192
The one on the bottom right.

508
00:18:17,193 --> 00:18:21,062
Gongju was rescued from an illegal dog breeding facility.

509
00:18:21,063 --> 00:18:22,503
- I see. - She was abused a lot.

510
00:18:22,703 --> 00:18:25,103
We tried to put her in a cage,

511
00:18:25,673 --> 00:18:27,243
she didn't want to go in.

512
00:18:27,973 --> 00:18:29,412
Because she didn't have any good memory in a cage.

513
00:18:29,413 --> 00:18:31,372
- Exactly. - She must've been really scared.

514
00:18:31,373 --> 00:18:33,213
- It became a trauma. - Of course it did.

515
00:18:34,113 --> 00:18:36,583
Goodness. So this baby is one year old.

516
00:18:36,953 --> 00:18:39,922
- That one is 1 year old too? - That one is 1 year and a half.

517
00:18:39,923 --> 00:18:42,352
- This one is nine months old. - Nine months? I see.

518
00:18:42,353 --> 00:18:43,952
Goodness. Come home with me.

519
00:18:43,953 --> 00:18:45,922
- How does it sleep like that? - Look at Nana sleeping.

520
00:18:45,923 --> 00:18:47,793
- It's because it's still a baby. - Oh, my.

